p.note:
. Cp. Vell. Pat. 2. 41.2; Plut. Caes. I. 1;
Val. Max. 9. 9. 1. Cinna was consul 87-84 B. C. The date of the
marriage was presumably before Cinna's death in 84 B. C.

. Mamercus Aemilius Lepidus Livianus
(cos. 77 B. C. with D. Junius Brutus; C. I. L. (ed. 2) i, p. 154);
cp. Cic. Brut. 175; Val. Max. 7. 7.6.

. For the custom of erecting maeniana or temporary
balconies over the buildings in the Forum cp. Festus 184.22;
Isidor. 15. 3. 11.

. Cicero's 'special constables'; cp. Cic. Att. 2. 1. 7;
Phil. 2. 16 (also adulescentes nobiles); Red. Sen. 12; Sall. Cat. 49. 4.
Plutarch (Caes. 8. 2) calls them merely Cicero's 'young men '.

div.p:
§ 5. induxit Vettium. Ursinus' correction of the MSS. inductum
is simpler on the whole than indicem subornavit Vettium inductum
(Roth). For Vettius cp. 17. 1. For this incident cp. Cic. Att. 2. 24;
Vat. 24; Sest. 132; Plut. Luc. 42. 7 (L. incriminated); App. 2. 12;
Dio 38. 9. Dio holds that there was a genuine plot, and that both
F2

Cic. ll. cc.; three speeches were extant in the Scholiast's time;
perhaps one for each of the three days on which the matter was
discussed (cp. triduo, below).

§ 2. reorum aut obaeratorum. Cp. 72 (grassatorum ope).
For Caesar's disreputable hangers-on cp. Cic. Att. 7. 3. 5; 9.18.2;
Fam. 8. 4. 2; 8. 14. 3; Plut. Caes. 51. 2.

p.note:
. The official residence in the precinct of Vesta;
it was made over by Augustus to the Vestal Virgins; part of the
atrium and mosaics are still visible; Platner, pp. 210-14.

div.p:
§ 1. abstinentiam. A just criticism, as is shown by his indemnities, confiscations, and forced loans. In Italy: Cic. Phil. 2. 64;
Fam. 13. 8. 2; Dio 42. 50. 2-3 (loans 47 B. C.). In Africa: B. Afr.
97.2-4. In Sardinia: B. Afr. 98. 3. In the East: Dio 42. 6. 3;
42. 49. 2-4 (48-47 B. C.). For Gaul and Spain see notes which
follow. The wealth displayed in his triumphs confirms this criticism; cp. note on diverso apparatu (37. 1). His wealth at death
was enormous; 700,000,000 of public money (Cic. Phil. 2. 93, etc.;
Vell. Pat. 2. 60.4; 100,000,000 private fortune (Plut. Cic. 43. 3;
Ant. 15).

p.note:
. A rare indication of first-hand research in Suetonius;
cp. Calig. 8. 2 ego in actis invenio; Aug. 5. 1; Tib. 5.
...

. Official dispatches, such as were required of provincial governors; cp. Cic. Prov. cons. 14; Pis. 39; Att. 5. 18. 1;
Fam. 15. 1 and 2 (specimens). These dispatches may well have
formed the basis of the Bellurn Gallicum.

. Cic. Phil. 2. 87; Liv. Epit. 116;
Plut. Caes. 57. 1; App. 2. 106; Dio 44.8.4; Grueber, Coins R.
Rep. in B.M. i, pp. 545-6 (DICT PERPET early in 44 B. С.);
C. I. L. (ed. 2), i, p. 61 caESAR DICT.in pERpPETVVM
(44 в. с.).

. On the Capitol next to the statue of
Brutus, founder of the Republic. Cp. Plin. 33.9-10; 34.22-3;
Cic. Deiot. 33; Dio 43. 45.3 (45 B. C.); Plut. Brut. 1. 3.

. Cp. 42. 1; Dio 41. 36. 3 (Transpadanes-
49 B. C.); Dio 41. 24. I (Gades-49 B.C.); cp. also Cic. Phil. 1. 24;
Meyer, Caesars Monarchie, pp. 483-96.

. About II a.m. The senate usually met early or
at dawn (Plut. Cic. 15. 3 and 19. 1; C. Gracch. 14. 1; Brut. 19. 2;
App. 2. 126).

. Cp. App. 2. 133-4 (they had showed their hostility to the murderers). Cp. Cic. Fam. II. 1. 1; Dio 44. 51. 1;
Cic. Phil. 2. 100-1. Antony kept the active troops under control,
but hurried off the veterans to their allotments.
